  "image_name": "06e4641cbb9a8aae261f99f79187c021.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how ocean warming sits at the center of interacting Earth-system processes and shows the broad consequences that spread through the atmosphere, land, ocean, cryosphere, and human systems. In the upper schematic, ocean warming is depicted as a committed change driven by anthropogenic forcing and embedded within three overlapping domains: the energy cycle, water cycle, and carbon cycle. Around it are key feedback loops linking warming to albedo and Earth’s energy imbalance, air–sea exchange, sea ice, freshwater, ocean transport, and ocean carbon, emphasizing that the ocean connects multiple parts of the climate system rather than responding in isolation.\n\nThe lower illustration translates these linkages into visible impacts across the planet. Upward orange arrows indicate widespread increases, including rainfall, tropical cyclone intensity, CO2 concentration, evaporation, sea level, marine heat waves, stratification, and coastal flooding and erosion. Green downward arrows mark decreases in variables such as sea ice, ice sheets, oxygen, salinity in some regions, and aspects of fisheries, aquaculture, and marine biodiversity. It also highlights saltwater intrusion, risks to coastal infrastructure, changes in ocean currents and density, and rising carbon acidity, showing ocean warming as a central driver of both environmental and societal change.",
